00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:15.800
then, you know, how much of that is going to impact? Should we sort of expect this to

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:22.740
be the level from which you can build based on when you get growth from, you know, younger

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:29.920
products in the portfolio while you, you know, get the profit growth through, you

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:36.800
know, SG&A management and, you know, R&D phasing, obviously, growth in second half.

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:41.860
So just in terms of gross margin trough point, should we be thinking about now or later?

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:50.360
Thanks for the question, Rajesh. So I think gross margin, we already said we had this

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:55.040
discussion beginning of the year, and I think it's the point on the gross margin where we

00:00:55.040 --> 00:01:05.080
are now is a good point to take from modeling for the future. And what we already said is

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:10.000
end of last year, Q3, Q4 of last year, if you take an average of that, that should be

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:12.920
the gross margin point that we take.

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:13.200
But

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:18.440
worth saying is that gross margin would never be flat. It depends on the profit mix that

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:24.600
we have from a quarter on quarter perspective. And as we move the portfolio forward, there

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:30.600
are pushes and pulls that we have in our portfolio. We've got a great drug if comes

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:38.720
to life like remibrutinib, a small molecule. We don't have any royalties versus some of

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:42.920
our other portfolio where the gross margins would be more stretched.

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:49.040
But I think from a modeling perspective, I would take the year to date gross margin

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:51.900
as a good indicator of what to expect for year to go.

00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:57.090
